<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Review problems on convergence and sums of infinite series
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-infinite-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-infinite-series" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems work with sequences and finite sums, and ask whether a series
converges and, when it does, what it sums to. If you would like to review this topic before attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-infinite-series" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-1">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>a, b, a+b</m> and <m>ab</m> are positive numbers that form 4
consecutive terms in a geometric sequence, find <m>a</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Since consecutive ratios in a geometric sequence are equal,
<md>
\frac{a}{b} = \frac{b}{a+b} = \frac{a+b}{ab}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
From the first equality,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{a}{b} = \frac{b}{a+b}
\;\Rightarrow\; a^2 + ab = b^2
\;\Rightarrow\; b^2 - ab - a^2 = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\;\overset{\div\, a^2}{\Rightarrow}\;
\left(\frac{b}{a}\right)^{2} - \left(\frac{b}{a}\right) - 1 = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{b}{a} = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{1+4}}{2}
\;\overset{\text{since } b/a \gt 0}{\Rightarrow}\;
\frac{b}{a} = \frac{1 + \sqrt5}{2}. \quad (\ast)</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
From the equality of the first and third fractions,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{a}{b} = \frac{a+b}{ab}
\;\Rightarrow\; a^2 = a + b
\;\overset{\div\, a}{\Rightarrow}\;
a \amp = 1 + \frac{b}{a}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\overset{(\ast)}{=}\; 1 + \frac{1+\sqrt5}{2}
= \frac{3 + \sqrt5}{2}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-10">
<statement>
<p>
The terms of a geometric series
<m>a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1} + \cdots</m> satisfy
<md>
a_5 + a_7 = 1500
\quad\text{and}\quad
a_{11} + a_{13} = 187500,
</md>
where <m>a_n = ar^{n-1}</m> denotes the <m>n</m>th term. Find
<m>a_1 + a_2 + a_3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using <m>a_n = ar^{n-1}</m>,
<md>
<mrow>a_5 + a_7 \amp = ar^4 + ar^6 = ar^4(1 + r^2) = 1500,</mrow>
<mrow>a_{11} + a_{13} \amp = ar^{10} + ar^{12} = ar^{10}(1 + r^2)
= 187500.</mrow>
</md>
Dividing the two equations,
<md>
\frac{a_{11} + a_{13}}{a_5 + a_7}
= \frac{ar^{10}(1+r^2)}{ar^{4}(1+r^2)}
= \frac{187500}{1500}
\;\Rightarrow\; r^6 = 125 = 5^3
\;\Rightarrow\; r^2 = 5.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Substituting back,
<md>
ar^4(1 + r^2) = 1500
\;\Rightarrow\; a(25)(1+5) = 1500
\;\Rightarrow\; a = \frac{1500}{150} = 10.
</md>
Since <m>r^2 = 5</m>, we have <m>r = \pm\sqrt5</m>, and therefore
<md>
<mrow>a_1 + a_2 + a_3 \amp = a + ar + ar^2 = a(1 + r + r^2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 10\left(1 \pm \sqrt5 + 5\right)
= 10\left(6 \pm \sqrt5\right).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-9">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the sequence defined by <m>a_1 = 1</m> and
<m>a_{n+1} = a_n + 3n^2 + 3n + 1</m>. Find a formula for <m>a_n</m> and
use it to compute <m>a_{200}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We compute the first few terms:
<md>
<mrow>n = 1: \quad a_2 \amp = a_1 + (3 + 3 + 1) = 1 + 7 = 8
\;\Rightarrow\; a_2 = 2^3</mrow>
<mrow>n = 2: \quad a_3 \amp = a_2 + (12 + 6 + 1) = 8 + 19 = 27
\;\Rightarrow\; a_3 = 3^3</mrow>
</md>
We notice that <m>a_1 = 1^3</m>, <m>a_2 = 2^3</m>, and
<m>a_3 = 3^3</m>, hence it seems that <m>a_n = n^3</m>.
</p>

<p>
Let us verify by computing <m>a_{n+1} - a_n</m> using
<m>a_n = n^3</m>:
<md>
<mrow>a_{n+1} - a_n \amp = (n+1)^3 - n^3</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = n^3 + 3n^2 + 3n + 1 - n^3
= 3n^2 + 3n + 1,</mrow>
</md>
which confirms that when <m>a_n = n^3</m>, then
<m>a_{n+1} = a_n + 3n^2 + 3n + 1</m>. Hence,
<md>
a_{200} = (200)^3 = 8 \times 10^{6}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-2">
<statement>
<p>
What is the sum of all multiples of 7 or 11 less than 1000?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that we need to subtract the multiples of <m>77</m>, since they
have been added twice:
<md>
<mrow>\amp \underbrace{(7 + 14 + 21 + \cdots + 77 + \cdots + 994)}_{\text{multiples of } 7}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \underbrace{(11 + 22 + \cdots + 77 + \cdots + 990)}_{\text{multiples of } 11}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad - \underbrace{(77 + 154 + \cdots + 924)}_{\text{multiples of } 77}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 7(1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + 142)
+ 11(1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + 90)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad - 77(1 + 2 + \cdots + 12)</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Using <m>\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n} i = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\amp = 7\,\frac{(142)(143)}{2}
+ 11\,\frac{(90)(91)}{2}
- 77\,\frac{(12)(13)}{2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 7(71)(143) + 11(45)(91) - 77(6)(13)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (7)(71)(13)(11) + (11)(13)(7)(45) - (7)(11)(6)(13)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (7)(11)(13)(71 + 45 - 6)
= (7)(11)(13)(110)
= 110110,</mrow>
</md>
where we used <m>143 = (13)(11)</m> and <m>91 = (13)(7)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-4">
<statement>
<p>
What can we conclude by applying the <m>n</m>th term test in the
following series?
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{3n}{\sqrt{6n^2 + 4n + 5}}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(5n+3)^{2n}}{(7n^2+2)^{n}}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{2\ln(n)}{3n}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{3n}{\sqrt{6n^2+4n+5}}
\amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{3n}{\sqrt6\, n}
= \sqrt{\frac32} \ne 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\; \text{the series diverges.}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{(5n+3)^{2n}}{(7n^2+2)^{n}}
\amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} \left(\frac{(5n+3)^2}{7n^2+2}\right)^{n}
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \left(\frac{25n^2 + 30n + 9}{7n^2+2}\right)^{n}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} \left(\frac{25}{7}\right)^{n} = \infty
\;\Rightarrow\; \text{the series diverges.}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{2\ln(n)}{3n}
\amp \;\overset{\text{H}}{=}\;
\frac23 \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{1/n}{1} = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\; \text{the } n\text{th Term Test is inconclusive.}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-5">
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the following sums or show that they diverge.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)(n+2)}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} e^{-2n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{10} e^{-2n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} e^{2n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{6}{(2n-1)(2n+1)}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{2n+1}{n^2(n+1)^2}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \ln\!\left(\frac{n}{2n+1}\right)</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(1 + \frac4n\right)^{n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{2}{3^n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{2^n}{3^n}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We use partial fractions:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{1}{n(n+1)(n+2)}
\amp = \frac{A}{n} + \frac{B}{n+1} + \frac{C}{n+2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{A(n+1)(n+2) + Bn(n+2) + Cn(n+1)}{n(n+1)(n+2)}.</mrow>
</md>
Setting <m>n = 0</m> gives <m>2A = 1</m>, so <m>A = \frac12</m>;
setting <m>n = -1</m> gives <m>-B = 1</m>, so <m>B = -1</m>; and
setting <m>n = -2</m> gives <m>2C = 1</m>, so <m>C = \frac12</m>.
Hence
<md>
<mrow>\amp \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)(n+2)}
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac12\left(\frac1n - \frac{2}{n+1}
+ \frac{1}{n+2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac12 \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)
- \frac12 \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\left(\frac{1}{n+1} - \frac{1}{n+2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac12 \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)
- \frac12 \sum_{n=2}^{\infty}
\left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac12\left(1 - \frac12\right)
+ \underbrace{\frac12 \sum_{n=2}^{\infty}
\left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)
- \frac12 \sum_{n=2}^{\infty}
\left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)}_{\text{zero}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac14.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
This is a geometric series with <m>a = 1</m> and
<m>r = \frac{1}{e^2}</m>:
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} e^{-2n}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{e^2}\right)^{n}
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{e^2}\right)^{n-1}
= \frac{1}{1 - \frac{1}{e^2}}
= \frac{e^2}{e^2 - 1}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
This is a finite geometric sum. Using
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{N} ar^{n-1} = a\,\frac{1-r^{N}}{1-r}</m>,
which was proved when we first studied geometric sums,
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{10} e^{-2n}
= \sum_{n=1}^{11} \left(\frac{1}{e^2}\right)^{n-1}
= \frac{1 - \left(e^{-2}\right)^{11}}{1 - e^{-2}}
= \frac{1 - e^{-22}}{1 - e^{-2}}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} e^{2n}
= \infty</m>, so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test the series diverges.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Partial fractions give
<md>
\frac{6}{(2n-1)(2n+1)} = \frac{A}{2n-1} + \frac{B}{2n+1}
= \frac{A(2n+1) + B(2n-1)}{(2n-1)(2n+1)},
</md>
and setting <m>n = \frac12</m> gives <m>2A = 6</m>, <m>A = 3</m>,
while <m>n = -\frac12</m> gives <m>-2B = 6</m>, <m>B = -3</m>. The sum
telescopes:
<md>
<mrow>s_k \amp = \left(\frac11 - \frac13\right)
+ \left(\frac13 - \frac15\right)
+ \left(\frac15 - \frac17\right)
+ \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left(\frac{1}{2k-1} - \frac{1}{2k+1}\right)
= 1 - \frac{1}{2k+1},</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \phantom{=} \text{so} \quad
3\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{2n-1} - \frac{1}{2n+1}\right)
= 3\lim_{k\to\infty} s_k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \phantom{=} \qquad
= 3\lim_{k\to\infty}\left(1 - \frac{1}{2k+1}\right) = 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Partial fractions (or direct observation) give
<md>
\frac{2n+1}{n^2(n+1)^2} = \frac{1}{n^2} - \frac{1}{(n+1)^2},
</md>
so the sum telescopes:
<md>
<mrow>s_k \amp = \sum_{n=1}^{k}
\left(\frac{1}{n^2} - \frac{1}{(n+1)^2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(1 - \frac14\right) + \left(\frac14 - \frac19\right)
+ \left(\frac19 - \frac{1}{16}\right) + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left(\frac{1}{k^2} - \frac{1}{(k+1)^2}\right)
= 1 - \frac{1}{(k+1)^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \Rightarrow \quad
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{2n+1}{n^2(n+1)^2}
= \lim_{k\to\infty} \left(1 - \frac{1}{(k+1)^2}\right) = 1.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \ln\!\left(\dfrac{n}{2n+1}\right)
= \ln\!\left(\dfrac12\right) \ne 0</m>, so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test
the series diverges.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Let <m>L = \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \left(1 + \frac4n\right)^{n}</m>.
Then
<md>
<mrow>\ln L \amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} n \ln\!\left(1 + \frac4n\right)
= \lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac{\ln\!\left(1 + \frac4n\right)}{\frac1n}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\overset{\text{H}}{=}\;
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac{\dfrac{-4/n^2}{1 + 4/n}}{-\dfrac{1}{n^2}}
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{4}{1 + \frac4n} = 4.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>L = e^4 \ne 0</m>, so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test the series
diverges.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<md>
\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{2}{3^n}
= \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} 2\left(\frac13\right)^{n}
= \frac{2}{1-\frac13} - \left(2 + \frac23\right)
= 3 - \frac83 = \frac13,
</md>
where the terms for <m>n = 0</m> and <m>n = 1</m> need to be
subtracted.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{2^n}{3^n}
\amp = \sum_{n=4}^{\infty} \left(\frac23\right)^{n-1}
= \frac{1}{1-\frac23}
- \left(\frac49 + \frac23 + 1\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3 - \frac{4+6+9}{9}
= 3 - \frac{19}{9}
= \frac{27-19}{9} = \frac89,</mrow>
</md>
where the subtracted terms correspond to <m>n = 3</m>, <m>n = 2</m>,
and <m>n = 1</m> of the shifted series.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-ser-3">
<statement>
<p>
The government has decided to give a $1,000 tax rebate to each
household in order to stimulate the economy. The government statistics
say that each household will spend 80% of the rebate in goods and
services. The businesses and individuals who benefited from that 80%
will then spend 80% of what they received and so on. The result is
called the multiplier effect. What is the total effect of the rebate on
the economy?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The rounds of spending form a geometric series with <m>a = 1000</m> and
<m>r = 0.8</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\amp 1000 + 0.8(1000) + (0.8)^2(1000) + \cdots
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 1000\,(0.8)^{n-1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1000}{1 - 0.8}
= \frac{1000}{0.2} = 5000 \text{ dollars}.</mrow>
</md>
The successive rounds of spending are shown in
<xref ref="fig-rev-multiplier"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-multiplier">
<caption>
The multiplier effect. Each round of spending is 80% of the previous
one, and the total of all the bars is
<m>\frac{1000}{1-0.8} = 5000</m> dollars.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-multiplier">
<diagram dimensions="(380,240)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.4,-160,11.0,1150)">
<line at="mult-xaxis" endpoints="((-0.5,0),(10.6,0))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<rectangle at="mult-b1" lower-left="(0.25,0)" dimensions="(0.7,1000)"
           fill="#0072B2" stroke="none"/>
