</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-1">
<statement>
<p>
Answer questions A and B below for the following infinite series:
<md>
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{6}{k^2} - \frac{6}{(k+1)^2}\right).
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Does the <m>n</m>th-term test apply? Remember to fully justify your
answer.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate the series or show that it diverges.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We compute the limit of the terms:
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{n\to\infty} a_n
\amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{6}{n^2} - \frac{6}{(n+1)^2}
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{6(n+1)^2 - 6n^2}{n^2(n+1)^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{12n + 6}{n^2(n+1)^2}
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{12n}{n^4} = 0.</mrow>
</md>
Hence the <m>n</m>th-term test does <em>not</em> apply: since the
limit of the terms is <m>0</m>, the test is inconclusive.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The series telescopes. The partial sum is
<md>
<mrow>s_n \amp = \left(\frac{6}{1^2} - \frac{6}{2^2}\right)
+ \left(\frac{6}{2^2} - \frac{6}{3^2}\right)
+ \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left(\frac{6}{(n-1)^2} - \frac{6}{n^2}\right)
+ \left(\frac{6}{n^2} - \frac{6}{(n+1)^2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 6 - \frac{6}{(n+1)^2},</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{6}{k^2} - \frac{6}{(k+1)^2}\right)
= \lim_{n\to\infty} s_n
= \lim_{n\to\infty} 6 - \frac{6}{(n+1)^2}
= 6 - 0 = 6.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-1">
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the series is convergent or divergent and if it is
convergent calculate its sum.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+1}\right) - \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+2}\right)</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1 + 5^n}{3^n}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The series telescopes. The partial sum is
<md>
<mrow>s_k \amp = \sum_{n=0}^{k}
\sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+1}\right) - \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sin(1) - \sin\!\left(\frac12\right)
+ \sin\!\left(\frac12\right) - \sin\!\left(\frac13\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \sin\!\left(\frac13\right) - \sin\!\left(\frac14\right)
+ \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{k+1}\right)
- \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{k+2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sin(1) - \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{k+2}\right).</mrow>
</md>
Hence the series converges and
<md>
<mrow>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+1}\right) - \sin\!\left(\frac{1}{n+2}\right)
\amp = \lim_{k\to\infty} s_k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sin(1) - \sin(0) = \sin(1).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We split the series into two geometric series:
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1 + 5^n}{3^n}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac13\right)^{n}
+ \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac53\right)^{n}.
</md>
Since <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \left(\frac53\right)^{n} = \infty</m>,
by the <m>n</m>th term test we can conclude that the series diverges.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-2">
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the series converges, and if so find its sum:
<md>
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{n}{n+5}\right)^{n}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that
<md>
\lim_{n\to\infty} a_n
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \left(1 + \frac5n\right)^{-n}
= \lim_{n\to\infty}
\left[\left(1 + \frac5n\right)^{\frac{n}{5}}\right]^{-5}
= e^{-5} \ne 0,
</md>
since <m>\left(1 + \frac5n\right)^{n/5} \to e</m>. Indeed, setting
<m>x = \frac{n}{5}</m> and
<m>L = \lim\limits_{x\to\infty}\left(1 + \frac1x\right)^{x}</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\ln L \amp = \lim_{x\to\infty} x \ln\!\left(1 + \frac1x\right)
= \lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{\ln\!\left(1 + \frac1x\right)}{\frac1x}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\overset{\text{H}}{=}\;
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{\dfrac{-1/x^2}{1 + 1/x}}{-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}
= \lim_{x\to\infty} \frac{1}{1 + \frac1x} = 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\; L = e.</mrow>
</md>
Therefore, the series diverges by the <m>n</m>th Term Test.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-3">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the following series. Answer the following questions.
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(x+4)^n}{2^n}
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the values of <m>x</m> for which the series converges.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the sum of the series for those values of <m>x</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We can write the series as a geometric series:
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(x+4)^n}{2^n}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{x+4}{2}\right)^{n}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{x}{2}+2\right)^{n},
</md>
which converges when
<m>\left|\frac{x}{2} + 2\right| \lt 1</m>:
<md>
-1 \lt \frac{x}{2} + 2 \lt 1
\;\Rightarrow\; -3 \lt \frac{x}{2} \lt -1
\;\Rightarrow\; -6 \lt x \lt -2,
</md>
i.e. the series converges on the interval <m>(-6, -2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
When <m>x \in (-6,-2)</m>, the geometric series with
<m>a = 1</m> and <m>r = \frac{x+4}{2}</m> gives
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{x+4}{2}\right)^{n}
= \frac{1}{1 - \frac{x+4}{2}}
= \frac{2}{2 - x - 4}
= -\frac{2}{x+2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-4">
<statement>
<p>
Express <m>8.81818181818\ldots</m> as a rational number, i.e. in the
form <m>\dfrac{p}{q}</m>, where <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are positive
integers with no common factors.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We write the repeating decimal as a geometric series:
<md>
8.81818181818\ldots
= 8 + \frac{81}{100} + \frac{81}{10000} + \cdots
= 8 + 81 \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{100}\right)^{n}.
</md>
Using the geometric series with <m>a = \frac{1}{100}</m> and
<m>r = \frac{1}{100}</m>,
<md>
8 + 81\left(\frac{\frac{1}{100}}{1 - \frac{1}{100}}\right)
= 8 + \frac{81}{99}
= 8 + \frac{9}{11}
= \frac{97}{11}.
</md>
Hence <m>p = 97</m> and <m>q = 11</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-8-b">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
A geometric series <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1}</m>
converges if and only if <m>|r| \le 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
As a counterexample consider
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 1^{n-1}</m>, which clearly
diverges even though <m>|r| = 1</m>. The correct condition is
<m>|r| \lt 1</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-2">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the series <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\frac{3^{k-1}}{5^k}</m>. Does the series converge? If so, what is the
result?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We first factor out <m>\frac13</m> so that the powers match:
<md>
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{3^{k-1}}{5^k}
= \frac13 \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{3^{k}}{5^k}
= \frac13 \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac35\right)^{k}.
</md>
Splitting off the <m>k = 0</m> term so that the sum starts at
<m>k = 1</m>,
<md>
\frac13 \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac35\right)^{k}
= \frac13\left[1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
\left(\frac35\right)\left(\frac35\right)^{k-1}\right].
</md>
</p>

<p>
The remaining sum is a geometric series with <m>a = \frac35</m> and
<m>r = \frac35</m>. Since <m>|r| = \frac35 \lt 1</m>, it converges and
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} ar^{k-1} = \frac{a}{1-r}</m>, so
<md>
\frac13\left[1 + \frac{\frac35}{1 - \frac35}\right]
= \frac13\left[1 + \frac32\right]
= \frac13 \cdot \frac52
= \frac56.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-4">
<statement>
<p>
Find the sum of all of the convergent series.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2}n\right)}{3^{n+1}}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{-2}{n(n+1)}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-25)^n}{(2n)!}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The factor <m>\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2}n\right)</m> cycles through
<m>0, 1, 0, -1</m> as <m>n = 0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots</m>, so only the odd
<m>n</m> contribute, with alternating signs. Writing
<m>n = 2k+1</m>,
<md>
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2}n\right)}{3^{n+1}}
= \frac{1}{3^2} - \frac{1}{3^4} + \frac{1}{3^6} - \cdots
= \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^k}{3^{2k+2}}
= \frac19 \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(-\frac19\right)^{k}.
</md>
This is a geometric series with <m>a = 1</m> and
<m>r = -\frac19</m>, and <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, so
<md>
\frac19 \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \left(-\frac19\right)}
= \frac19 \cdot \frac{1}{\frac{10}{9}}
= \frac{1}{10}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
By the constant multiple rule,
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{-2}{n(n+1)}
= -2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)}</m>. The remaining series
telescopes: since
<m>\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)} = \dfrac1n - \dfrac{1}{n+1}</m>,
<md>
s_n = \left(1 - \frac12\right) + \left(\frac12 - \frac13\right)
+ \cdots + \left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)
= 1 - \frac{1}{n+1} \;\longrightarrow\; 1.
</md>
Hence the sum is <m>-2(1) = -2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recall <m>\cos x = \displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\frac{(-1)^n x^{2n}}{(2n)!}</m>. With <m>x = 5</m>,
<md>
\cos(5) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n 5^{2n}}{(2n)!}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^n 25^{n}}{(2n)!}
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-25)^{n}}{(2n)!},
</md>
so the sum is <m>\cos(5)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
